## Physics

(1) Answer : (4)
Hint:
Use $F_{E}=q E$ and $\Sigma F=0$ for equilibrium.
Solution:
From the F.B.D.

![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/2026_02_05_fbd_q1.jpg?height=200&width=180)

$T \sin \theta=q E$

$T \cos \theta=m g$

$\tan \theta=\frac{q E}{m g}$

$\frac{m g \tan \theta}{q}=\frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_{0}}$

$\sigma=\frac{\varepsilon_{0}}{q} m g \tan \theta$

$=\frac{\varepsilon_{0}}{10^{-6}} \times 10^{-6} \times 10 \times 1$

$=10 \varepsilon_{0}$

(2) Answer : (3)
Hint:
$q_{\text {in }}=\varepsilon_{0}$ (net electric flux)
Solution:

![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/2026_02_05_cube_sol_q2.jpg?height=160&width=200)

$\vec{E}_{\text {left }}=\alpha a^{\frac{1}{3}} \hat{i}$

$\vec{E}_{\text {right }}=\alpha(2 a)^{\frac{1}{3}} \hat{i}$

Now net flux through the cube is $\phi_{\text {net }}=\phi_{\text {right }}+\phi_{\text {left }}$

$=a^{2}\left(\alpha(2 a)^{\frac{1}{3}}-\alpha a^{\frac{1}{3}}\right)$

$=\alpha a^{\frac{7}{3}}\left(2^{\frac{1}{3}}-1\right)$

Now, $q=\alpha \varepsilon_{0} a^{\frac{7}{3}}\left(2^{\frac{1}{3}}-1\right)$

$=400 \varepsilon_{0}\left(2^{\frac{1}{3}}-1\right)$

