Solution:

$E_{\text {ring }}=\frac{K Q x}{\left(R^{2}+x^{2}\right)^{3 / 2}}$

$\frac{d E}{d x}=0 \Rightarrow x=\frac{R}{\sqrt{2}}$

$\therefore$ At $x=\frac{R}{\sqrt{2}} \Rightarrow E_{\max }$

(4) Answer : (2)
Hint:
In charge redistribution, outer surfaces of both outermost metal plates always get half of total charge equally distributed.
Total charge on each plate will also remain constant.
Solution:

$\phi_{0}=\frac{\text { Total charge }}{2}=\frac{-Q+3 Q+Q}{2}=\frac{3}{2} Q$

On outer faces of plate 1 and 3
By charge conservation

![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/2026_02_05_plates_sol_q4.jpg?height=200&width=180)

$\frac{3 Q}{2}+x=-Q \quad \therefore x=-\frac{5}{2} Q$

Charge on plate 2 :

Upper face has charge $=-x=\frac{5 Q}{2}$

By charge conservation $\frac{5 Q}{2}+y=3 Q$

$y=3 Q-\frac{5 Q}{2}=\frac{Q}{2}$

(5) Answer : (2)
Hint:

$U=\frac{Q^{2}}{2 C}$ and $1.44 U=\frac{(Q+2)^{2}}{2 C}$

Solution:

$\therefore 1.44 \frac{Q^{2}}{2 C}=\frac{(Q+2)^{2}}{2 C}$

$\therefore 1.44=\frac{(Q+2)^{2}}{Q^{2}}$

$1.2=\frac{Q+2}{Q}$

$\therefore 1.2 Q=Q+2$

$0.2 Q=2$

$Q=\frac{2}{0.2}=10 \mathrm{C}$

(6) Answer : (3)
Hint:
Energy stored in a capacitor $=\frac{q^{2}}{2 C}=\frac{1}{2} C V^{2}$
Solution:
Energy loss in charge sharing

$\Delta U=\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{C_{1} \times C_{2}}{C_{1}+C_{2}} \times\left(V_{1}-V_{2}\right)^{2}$

$\Delta U=\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{C \times 4 C}{(C+4 C)} \times(V+2 V)^{2}$

$=\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{4}{5} \times 9 C V^{2}=\frac{18}{5} C V^{2}$

(7) Answer : (4)
Hint:

$U=\frac{Q^{2}}{2 C}$ and $1.21 U=\frac{(Q+2)^{2}}{2 C}$

Solution:

$\therefore 1.21 \frac{Q^{2}}{2 C}=\frac{(Q+2)^{2}}{2 C}$

$\therefore 1.21=\frac{(Q+2)^{2}}{Q^{2}}$

$1.1=\frac{Q+2}{Q}$

$\therefore 1.1 Q=Q+2$

$0.1 Q=2$

$Q=\frac{2}{0.1}=20 \mathrm{C}$

(8) Answer : (3)
Hint:
When battery remains connected, potential difference across the plates remains constant.
Solution:

Since the capacitor remains connected to the battery, the potential difference $V$ between the plates is maintained constant by the battery. When distance $d$ is increased:

$C=\frac{\varepsilon_{0} A}{d}$ decreases

$Q=C V$ decreases (since $C$ decreases)

$U=\frac{1}{2} C V^{2}$ decreases

Only the potential difference remains constant.
